**Vibecoder PRD (Product Requirements Document)**  
**Version:** 1.0  
**Date:** April 2026  
**Author:** Grok (assisted by Jung Rama)  
**Tech Stack (mandated):** Bun (runtime) + Nuxt 4 (full-stack)  
**Goal:** Give Cursor (or any AI coding agent) a single, crystal-clear document so it can build **exactly** what you described without deviation.

---

### 1. Product Vision

**Vibecoder** is an AI-first web app that turns a vague “vibe/idea” into a fully documented, actionable MVP in minutes.  
It replaces chaotic brainstorming with a structured interview → documentation → ordered task list → agent-ready prompts pipeline.

**Tagline:** “From vibe to shipped. Step by step. No more staring at a blank editor.”

---

### 2. Target User

- Solo developers / indie hackers / “vibe coders”
- Product people who have ideas but struggle with structure
- Teams that want a single source of truth before touching Cursor/Claude

---

### 3. Core User Flow (High-Level)

1. **New Project** → Interview (static form + dynamic AI chat)
2. **AI generates documentation in 3 distinct model hits** (Project + Goals → Design Guidelines → Tech Stack)
3. **User approves / iterates**
4. **Generate ordered MVP Task List**
5. **User edits or asks AI to regenerate/adjust**
6. **“Add Prompt to Task”** for every task (AI-generated agent-ready prompt)
7. **Export / Connect** → Cursor, Claude, or any external AI agent can pick up the tasks + prompts (this is the “MCP” feature)

---

### 4. Detailed Requirements

#### 4.1 Phase 1 – Interview & Documentation Generation (Your priority – fully detailed here)

**4.1.1 Interview Process**

**Static Questions (first screen – form, mandatory):**

1. Project Title / Working Name
2. One-sentence vibe / elevator pitch
3. Detailed description (textarea, min 100 words encouraged)
4. Target users / audience (multi-select + “other” free text)
   - Examples: Developers, Designers, Students, Small businesses, Gen-Z, etc.
5. Core problem this solves (what pain point?)
6. Main success metric / goal of the project (what does “done” look like?)
7. **Design Vibe Selector** (this is what you meant by “what user select at first”)
   - Cards with previews: Minimal, Vibrant, Professional, Playful, Futuristic, Brutalist, Glassmorphic, Retro, Dark Academia, etc.
   - User can pick 1 primary + up to 2 secondary vibes
8. Any hard constraints? (time, budget, preferred platforms: web/mobile/desktop, existing tech you love/hate)
9. Any existing assets? (Figma link, repo, Notion, etc.)

**Dynamic Questions (AI Interviewer – conversational chat after static form):**
After user submits static answers, the app opens a **real-time AI chat** (streaming via OpenRouter).

**AI Rules for dynamic questions (hard-coded system prompt):**

- Ask maximum 3–5 clarifying questions per turn.
- Never ask generic questions — always reference previous answers.
- Goal = fill gaps in scope, priorities, edge cases, non-functional requirements, user journey, success criteria.
- Examples of dynamic questions the AI will generate:
  - “You mentioned the target is ‘small businesses’. Are they non-technical? Should the UI be extremely simple?”
  - “You want it built fast — are you open to using Supabase/Firebase or do you prefer self-hosted Postgres?”
  - “You said ‘vibrant’ design. Should we lean more toward playful illustrations or bold typography + gradients?”
  - “Any must-have features vs nice-to-have? Please rank the top 3 features you described.”
- User can type “done” or click “Finish Interview” at any time → AI summarizes what it learned and asks for final confirmation.

**4.1.2 Documentation Generation (3 distinct model hits)**

After interview ends, user clicks **“Generate Documentation”**.

The backend orchestrates **exactly 3 separate OpenRouter calls** for quality:

**Hit 1 – Project Overview & Main Goals**  
Model call #1 → Output:

- Project Name (polished)
- One-paragraph description
- Main Goal + 3–5 Success Metrics
- Key Stakeholders / Users

**Hit 2 – Design Guidelines**  
Model call #2 (receives Hit 1 + original design vibe selection + interview data) → Output:

- Color palette (with hex codes + rationale)
- Typography scale
- Component library recommendations (shadcn/ui, Nuxt UI, etc.)
- Layout philosophy
- Accessibility & motion guidelines
- Moodboard-style description

**Hit 3 – Tech Stack**  
Model call #3 (receives previous two outputs + constraints) → Output:

- Full recommended stack (frontend, backend, DB, auth, deployment, etc.)
- Why each choice (cost, speed, maintainability, your existing skills)
- Folder structure recommendation
- Any third-party services

**UI for this stage:**

- Three tabs/sections that populate one by one (with loading skeletons).
- Each section has **“Regenerate”** button (triggers only that hit again).
- User can edit any text directly (rich markdown editor).
- Final **“Approve Documentation”** button (locks the brief).

#### 4.2 Phase 2 – MVP Task List

After approval:

- Big button **“Generate MVP Task List”**
- AI creates ordered, dependency-aware list (e.g. 15–30 tasks).
- Each task contains:
  - Title
  - Description
  - Acceptance criteria (bullet list)
  - Estimated effort (small/medium/large)
  - Dependencies (other task IDs)

User can:

- Drag & drop to reorder
- Add / delete / edit tasks
- Click **“Regenerate entire list”** or **“Ask AI to adjust this section”** (chat opens with context of current list)

#### 4.3 Phase 3 – “Add a Prompt to Task”

For every task there is a button **“Add Prompt to Task”** (or “Enhance for Agent”).

- AI generates a **highly detailed, agent-ready prompt** that Cursor / Claude / Windsurf / etc. can consume directly.
- Prompt includes:
  - Exact task context from the approved documentation
  - Relevant design guidelines
  - Tech stack rules
  - File structure references
  - Acceptance criteria as test checklist
  - “You are an expert full-stack engineer using Bun + Nuxt 4…”

User can edit the prompt before saving.

#### 4.4 MCP / External AI Agent Integration (the “mcp” you mentioned)

**Feature name in UI:** “Connect to Cursor / Claude / Agents”

**Requirements:**

- Export entire project as **single JSON file** (`project-vibecoder-export.json`) containing:
  - Documentation (all three sections)
  - Task list (ordered)
  - Every task + its agent prompt
- **Optional REST API endpoint** (`/api/projects/:id/tasks`) that external agents can poll:
  - GET next uncompleted task + its prompt
  - POST `/complete` to mark task done and get next one
- This enables Cursor, Claude Projects, custom agents, or any “MCP-compatible” tool to connect and start executing automatically.

(If “MCP” refers to a specific protocol you know — please clarify the exact acronym/spec and I’ll refine this section.)

